### 4.3 Redis

**What it is.** An in-memory key-value store. Every read and write is served
from RAM, so operations take well under a millisecond.

**Why it is here.** Shopping carts. cart-service (Node.js) stores each cart as
a Redis **hash** under the key `cart:<userId>` (one field per product) and
sets a **1-hour expiry**, so abandoned carts clean themselves up.

**Real-world use cases.** Caching database results, user sessions, shopping
carts, rate limiting, leaderboards, distributed locks, simple queues and
pub/sub.

**Configuration here.**

```yaml
redis:
  image: redis:7-alpine
  healthcheck:
    test: ["CMD", "redis-cli", "ping"]
```

No volume on purpose: carts are temporary. A restart empties all carts, which
is acceptable for a demo (in production you would enable persistence or use a
managed service with replication).

**How to use it.**

```bash
docker compose exec redis redis-cli
```
```
KEYS cart:*            # demo only — use SCAN in production, KEYS blocks Redis
HGETALL cart:<userId>  # contents of one cart
TTL cart:<userId>      # seconds until it expires
INFO memory
MONITOR                # live stream of every command (debug only)
```

**Observed by.** [redis-exporter](#59-redis-exporter) → *Infrastructure*
dashboard (commands/sec, memory). Every Redis command appears as a span under
cart-service.

**Common problems.**
- *Carts empty after a restart* — expected, see above.
- *Memory keeps growing* — keys without TTL; check with `INFO keyspace`.

**Alternatives.** Valkey (open-source Redis fork), Memcached (cache only),
Amazon ElastiCache / MemoryDB.

