### 4.4 RabbitMQ

**What it is.** A message broker. Services *publish* messages to an
**exchange**; the exchange routes copies into **queues** according to
**bindings**; other services *consume* from those queues. Producer and consumer
never talk to each other directly and do not even need to be running at the
same time.

**Why it is here.** Checkout is asynchronous: the order is accepted immediately,
and payment, stock and notifications happen afterwards through events. If
payment-service is down, the messages simply wait in its queue.

**Topology in this project** (exchange `ecommerce.events`, type **topic**):

| Queue | Binding (routing key) | Consumer | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| `payment.order-created` | `order.created` | payment-service | Charge the order |
| `order.payment-results` | `payment.*` | order-service | Mark the order paid / failed |
| `inventory.payment-completed` | `payment.completed` | inventory-service | Reduce stock |
| `notification.all-events` | `order.*`, `payment.*` | notification-service | Send notifications |

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  order[order-service] -- order.created --> ex{{ecommerce.events<br/>topic}}
  payment[payment-service] -- "payment.completed /<br/>payment.failed" --> ex
  ex --> q1[payment.order-created] --> payment
  ex --> q2[order.payment-results] --> order
  ex --> q3[inventory.payment-completed] --> inventory[inventory-service]
  ex --> q4[notification.all-events] --> notify[notification-service]
```

A *topic* exchange matches routing keys with wildcards: `payment.*` matches
both `payment.completed` and `payment.failed`.

**Real-world use cases.** Order pipelines, background jobs (emails, PDFs,
image processing), decoupling microservices, smoothing traffic spikes, retry
and dead-letter handling.

**Configuration here.**

```yaml
rabbitmq:
  image: rabbitmq:3.13-management-alpine   # includes the web UI
  environment:
    RABBITMQ_DEFAULT_USER: ${RABBITMQ_USER:-guest}
    RABBITMQ_DEFAULT_PASS: ${RABBITMQ_PASSWORD:-guest}
  ports:
    - "15672:15672"                         # management UI
  healthcheck:
    test: ["CMD", "rabbitmq-diagnostics", "-q", "ping"]
```

- AMQP (port 5672) is only available inside the network; services connect with
  `amqp://guest:guest@rabbitmq:5672`.
- The **`rabbitmq_prometheus` plugin** is already enabled in this image and
  serves metrics on port **15692** — so no separate exporter is needed.
- The exchange and queues are declared by the services at startup, not by
  RabbitMQ configuration.

**How to use it.** Open <http://localhost:15672> (guest / guest):
- **Queues** tab — *Ready* (waiting) and *Unacked* (being processed) counts.
  A growing *Ready* number means the consumer is down or too slow.
- **Connections** tab — which service is connected. This is the only place to
  see client identity; Prometheus metrics do not carry it.
- **Exchanges → ecommerce.events → Bindings** — the routing table above.

**Observed by.** Prometheus scrapes two endpoints of the plugin:
`/metrics` (job `rabbitmq`, broker-wide totals) and
`/metrics/detailed?family=queue_coarse_metrics` (job `rabbitmq-queues`,
per-queue depth). The *Infrastructure* dashboard shows messages ready per
queue, publish/deliver rates and connections/consumers. In Jaeger, a single
checkout trace continues *through* RabbitMQ, because the services propagate
the trace context in message headers.

**Try it.** `docker compose stop rabbitmq` → checkouts slow down or fail;
watch the RED dashboard and Jaeger. `docker compose start rabbitmq` → queued
work drains.

**Common problems.**
- *Queue depth climbing* — consumer crashed; check its logs in Grafana.
- *Unacked stuck high* — consumer receives but never acknowledges (bug or hang).
- *Container exits at boot with `failed_to_parse_configuration_file`* — a
  mounted config file is unreadable (e.g. cloud-synced folder); this repo
  mounts none, but keep it in mind if you add one.

**Alternatives.** Apache Kafka (high-volume event streaming, replay), Amazon
SQS/SNS, Amazon MQ, NATS, Redis Streams.

