## 5. Observability pipeline

### 5.1 OpenTelemetry Collector

**What it is.** A vendor-neutral agent/gateway from the OpenTelemetry project.
It **receives** telemetry, **processes** it, and **exports** it to one or more
back-ends. The `-contrib` image includes the community components (like the
`spanmetrics` connector and the Prometheus exporter).

**Why it is here.** It is the single front door for all application telemetry.
The 8 services are written in 5 languages; they all send OTLP to the same place,
and none of them knows that Jaeger or Prometheus exist.

**Real-world use cases.** Avoiding vendor lock-in; sending the same data to two
vendors during a migration; dropping or redacting sensitive attributes before
data leaves the network; sampling to cut cost; adding environment/cluster
labels centrally.

**The pipeline here** (`observability/otel-collector.yaml`):

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  otlp[receiver: otlp<br/>:4317 gRPC / :4318 HTTP] --> tp[traces pipeline]
  tp -- memory_limiter, batch --> j[exporter: otlp/jaeger]
  tp --> sm[connector: spanmetrics]
  otlp --> mp[metrics pipeline]
  sm --> mp
  mp -- memory_limiter, batch --> pe[exporter: prometheus<br/>:8889]
  otlp --> lp[logs pipeline] --> dbg[exporter: debug]
```

| Block | Setting | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `receivers.otlp` | gRPC 4317, HTTP 4318 | Accepts OTLP from the SDKs (apps use HTTP: `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://otel-collector:4318`) |
| `processors.memory_limiter` | 400 MiB limit, 100 MiB spike | Refuses data instead of crashing when memory is tight. Always first. |
| `processors.batch` | 5 s | Groups data into batches — fewer, larger requests to back-ends |
| `connectors.spanmetrics` | buckets 5 ms … 10 s; dimensions `http.request.method`, `http.response.status_code`; exemplars on | **Builds RED metrics from spans** (see below) |
| `exporters.otlp/jaeger` | `jaeger:4317`, TLS off | Sends traces to Jaeger |
| `exporters.prometheus` | `:8889`, OpenMetrics on | Exposes metrics for Prometheus to scrape; OpenMetrics is required for exemplars |
| `exporters.debug` | basic | Prints a summary to the collector's own stdout |
| `extensions.health_check` | `:13133` | Liveness endpoint |

**Why the `spanmetrics` connector matters.** Each language's OTel library
emits its own HTTP metrics, and they disagree on names and units (older
`http_server_duration_milliseconds` vs newer `http_server_request_duration_seconds`).
The connector instead computes metrics from the **spans** — which look the same
in every language — so every service gets identical metrics:

- `traces_span_metrics_calls_total` — request count (with `status_code` for errors)
- `traces_span_metrics_duration_milliseconds_bucket` — latency histogram

Both carry `service_name`, `span_name`, `span_kind`, plus the two dimensions
above. The RED dashboard and Jaeger's *Monitor* tab are built entirely on them.
A connector is both an exporter (of the traces pipeline) and a receiver (of the
metrics pipeline) — that is how one signal is turned into another.

**About logs.** Applications here log to stdout, which Promtail ships to Loki.
The collector's logs pipeline only receives OTLP logs (if a service sends any)
and prints them via `debug`. To make the collector the single path for logs
too, add a Loki/OTLP exporter to that pipeline.

**How to use it.**
```bash
docker compose logs -f otel-collector                  # export errors appear here
docker compose exec prometheus wget -qO- http://otel-collector:8889/metrics | head
```

**Adding a vendor** (e.g. New Relic, Elastic, Datadog) = add an exporter and
append it to the pipelines. No application changes:

```yaml
exporters:
  otlphttp/newrelic:
    endpoint: https://otlp.eu01.nr-data.net
    headers:
      api-key: ${env:NEW_RELIC_LICENSE_KEY}
service:
  pipelines:
    traces:
      exporters: [otlp/jaeger, spanmetrics, otlphttp/newrelic]
```

**Common problems.**
- *Edited the YAML, nothing changed* — mounted config is read at start:
  `docker compose restart otel-collector`.
- *No data anywhere* — collector failed to start because of a YAML error;
  check its logs first.
- *A service is missing* — its `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` is wrong, or the
  container was not recreated after the variable was added
  (`docker compose up -d --force-recreate <service>`).
- *`unknown_service:…` appears* — an SDK without `OTEL_SERVICE_NAME`.

**Alternatives.** Grafana Alloy, Fluent Bit, Vector, vendor agents (Datadog
Agent, New Relic infrastructure agent).

