### 5.4 Loki

**What it is.** Grafana's log database. Unlike Elasticsearch it does **not**
index the full text of every line — it indexes only a small set of **labels**
(here `service` and `container`) and stores the compressed lines. Searching
inside lines is done at query time. That makes it much cheaper to run.

**Why it is here.** To keep every container's logs in one searchable place,
linked to traces.

**Real-world use cases.** Centralised application and Kubernetes logs,
incident debugging, audit trails, log-based alerts — especially when cost
matters more than full-text search speed.

**Configuration here.**

```yaml
loki:
  image: grafana/loki:3.2.0
  command: ["-config.file=/etc/loki/local-config.yaml"]
```

- Uses the **built-in sample config** of the image: single process, no auth,
  filesystem storage, TSDB index (schema v13).
- **No volume and no host port**: logs are lost if the container is
  recreated, and Loki is reached through Grafana (Explore → Loki) or by
  other containers at `http://loki:3100`.

**How to use it.** Grafana → **Explore** → data source **Loki**. LogQL examples:

```logql
{service="order-service"}                                   # all logs of one service
{service=~".+"} |= "error"                                   # any line containing "error"
{service="payment-service"} |~ "(?i)declined"                # case-insensitive regex
{service=~".+"} |= "4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736"        # every line of one trace
sum by (service) (count_over_time({service=~".+"} |= "error" [5m]))   # errors per service as a metric
```

Always start a query with a label selector `{...}` — that is what Loki uses to
find the right data quickly.

**Common problems.**
- *No logs* — Promtail is not running or cannot reach Loki
  (`docker compose logs promtail`).
- *Logs gone after restart* — no volume (see above).
- *"Too many outstanding requests" / slow queries* — narrow the time range and
  the label selector.

**Alternatives.** Elasticsearch / OpenSearch (+ Kibana), Graylog, Splunk,
vendor log products; managed: Grafana Cloud Logs, CloudWatch Logs.

