### 5.7 cAdvisor

**What it is.** *Container Advisor* from Google: reads resource usage of every
running container from the kernel (cgroups) and exposes it as Prometheus
metrics.

**Why it is here.** To answer "which container is using the CPU / memory?" —
application metrics cannot see this.

**Real-world use cases.** Container right-sizing, spotting memory leaks before
an OOM kill, noisy-neighbour detection. In Kubernetes the same code runs inside
every kubelet.

**Configuration here.**

```yaml
cadvisor:
  image: ghcr.io/google/cadvisor:v0.60.5
  privileged: true                         # needs kernel-level access
  command: ["--docker_only=true", "--housekeeping_interval=15s"]
  volumes:
    - /:/rootfs:ro
    - /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock:ro
    - /run/containerd/containerd.sock:/run/containerd/containerd.sock:ro
    - /sys:/sys:ro
    - /var/lib/docker/:/var/lib/docker:ro
```

- `--docker_only` — report containers only, not every system cgroup.
- `--housekeeping_interval=15s` — collect at the same pace Prometheus scrapes.
- On **Docker Desktop (macOS/Windows)** the paths refer to Docker's Linux VM,
  not your laptop. Both sockets are mounted explicitly; the containerd socket
  is needed when Docker uses the containerd image store.

Useful metrics: `container_cpu_usage_seconds_total`,
`container_memory_working_set_bytes`, `container_network_receive_bytes_total`.
The label `container_label_com_docker_compose_service` holds the Compose
service name.

**Common problems.** *No per-container series* — usually a socket/mount
mismatch on Docker Desktop; check `docker compose logs cadvisor`.
`privileged: true` is a security trade-off — acceptable locally, review it
elsewhere.

**Alternatives.** node-exporter (host-level), Docker's own metrics endpoint,
kubelet/cAdvisor in Kubernetes, vendor infrastructure agents.

